# The Final Journey

## 3 August 2025 — A Day That Began Like Any Other

On **3 August 2025**, an ordinary day began in our family.

That morning, Mr. Balarama Krishna woke up early. At around **6:00 AM**, he completed his morning routine, attended to his personal needs, took his bath, performed his **pooja**, and then left for work.

He left home **without having breakfast**. He told his wife to prepare breakfast and said that he would return, have it, and then continue with his day.

One of the places where he most frequently worked was **Stambalagaruvu, Guntur**. It was an area where our family had initially settled after moving to Guntur in **2020**. By this time, however, we were staying in **Krishna Nagar, Guntur**.

That morning, he went to Stambalagaruvu for work.

He had no idea that this ordinary morning would become the final journey of his life.

---

## The Accident

After completing his work, Balarama Krishna began his journey back home on his **bicycle**.

He was travelling along the **third lane of a six-lane, two-way road**. While riding on one side of the road, he decided to shift toward the left and move into the adjacent lane.

At that exact moment, everything changed.

All of a sudden, a car struck the **rear tyre of his bicycle**.

The impact was so strong that he was thrown into the air. He landed on the **bonnet of the car**, and his head struck the **windshield with tremendous force**.

The impact was severe enough to create a **large hole in the windshield** of the car.

Immediately after the collision, the car driver applied the brakes suddenly. Due to the force generated by the sudden braking, Balarama Krishna was thrown from the bonnet and fell back onto the road.

The back of his head struck the road with considerable force.

There was **not much external bleeding**.

The car driver and another person immediately got out of the vehicle and tried to help him. At that moment, he was still **conscious**.

When they asked him his name, **he was able to respond**.

They initially tried to call an ambulance. However, they were told that it would take some time to arrive. So they decided to take him to **GGH, Guntur**, in the same car.

During the journey to the hospital, he gradually became drowsy. He slowly began to fall asleep and was **snoring**.

The people accompanying him tried repeatedly to wake him and speak with him so that he would remain conscious until they reached the hospital.

When they arrived at GGH, he was moved from the car onto a **stretcher** and taken into the **emergency ward**.

---

## The News Reached Us

At that time, we knew nothing about what had happened.

We were still at home.

My mother, unaware of the seriousness of the situation, was actually scolding us, saying something like:

> "Even at this age, your father doesn't want to stay at home. He keeps going outside and doing something."

While this conversation was taking place, a stranger came to our home.

He told us:

> **"Babu, your father met with an accident. Don't panic. He has been shifted to the hospital. Go and meet him."**

We did not have even a moment to think.

Without imagining that anything major had happened, we immediately left for the hospital.

At that time, we were thinking that it might be **just a small accident**—perhaps nothing serious, maybe not even a fracture.

We had no idea what awaited us.

---

## At GGH

When we reached the hospital and saw the situation, we were completely shocked.

We had no words.

We called out:

**"Nanna..."**

He had always responded when we called him—even with a single call.

But this time, **there was no response**.

His pulse appeared to be normal. Everything seemed normal from the outside, yet **he was not responding to us**.

The doctors asked us to sign a consent form for **high-risk treatment**.

Our hands were trembling.

We were struggling to speak.

After he was placed on **ventilatory support**, he was taken for an **MRI scan**.

After examining him, the doctors told us that his condition was **critical**.

There were no tears at that moment.

We were simply holding on to the hope that nothing would happen to him—that somehow he would recover and come back to us normally.

After spending several hours at GGH, we decided to move him to a **private hospital** in the hope of obtaining further specialized treatment.

---

## The Private Hospital

We took him to a **Lalitha super-speciality hospital**, where he was admitted to the emergency ward.

The necessary tests were performed.

By evening, the **neurosurgeon and neurologist** examined him and reviewed all of his reports.

We were still in shock.

We were desperately hoping to hear something reassuring from the doctors.

Then the doctor came and spoke with us.

The words we heard were deeply frightening.

The doctor explained that his head had been struck with tremendous force and that there was **internal bleeding inside the brain**.

His condition was extremely serious.

The doctor told us that there was approximately a **95% chance that he would not survive**.

Our hearts broke at those words.

It felt as though everything had collapsed in an instant.

The doctor told us that they would continue to monitor him until the following morning, holding on to that remaining **5% chance**.

We prayed for him.

We continued to believe that he would come back to us.

We held on to hope.

---

# 4 August 2025 — The Morning We Never Wanted

We did not sleep that night.

We waited.

We prayed.

We kept hoping.

Then morning came.

It was **4 August 2025**.

Time continued to pass.

Eventually, we were called to speak with the doctors.

Our hearts were beating faster and faster.

We knew we were about to hear something important, but we were terrified of what those words might be.

The **Head of the Department of Neurology** spoke with us.

And then came the words that no family ever wants to hear.

She told us:

> **"We tried in all possible ways, but Mr. Balarama Krishna is not responding. He is brain dead."**

With those words, our hearts sank.

She explained that a final **apnea test** would be performed to confirm brain death. After that, we would be allowed to see him.

When we entered the ICU and saw him lying there on the **ventilator**, we broke down completely.

We cried like never before.

It was impossible to accept what we were seeing.

---

## Returning to GGH

After that, we spoke with the hospital supervisor.

She explained the situation to us and advised us that if we continued to keep him in the private hospital, the hospital expenses would continue to increase, while his organs would gradually deteriorate. She told us that there would be no meaningful benefit in continuing there and asked us to make a decision.

With immense pain and sorrow in our hearts, we decided to move him back to the **government hospital, GGH, Guntur**.

We shifted him back to GGH that evening.

He was lying on a **stretcher with ventilatory support**, and we remained beside him with overwhelming grief and pain in our hearts.

---

## The Night

At that point, it was difficult to comprehend what was happening.

His organs were still functioning.

He was physically alive.

Yet he was unable to breathe on his own and was **not responding to treatment**.

During the night, his **pulse began fluctuating**.

At times, when the doctors checked with a **stethoscope**, they could not hear the pulse clearly. Yet when they checked with the **ECG**, the heartbeat was still being detected.

It felt as though he was still fighting to survive.

There was **no major external wound on his body**.

There was no significant injury visible anywhere else.

The damage was concentrated entirely in his head, with the **brain being particularly affected**.

We kept wishing that somehow this was only a nightmare.

We wished that we would wake up and find that none of it had happened.

But it wasn't a nightmare.

**It was real.**

And this was the final journey of **Kanagala Balarama Krishna**.

